 succeeded in 65ms:
   1: # SPEC: Phase 6 — Meeting Notes
   2: 
   3: Goal: Company members can record, revise, search, and export meeting notes per space — with action items, 
version history, and an AI-generated draft summary and action items from an uploaded transcript using the company's 
own AI provider key.
   4: 
   5: ## 1. Summary
   6: 
   7: Virtual Office gains a meeting-notes capability. A member writes a note for a
   8: space (title, meeting date, participants, rich-text content), tracks action
   9: items, and can revise, restore, delete, search, and export notes. A member can
  10: upload a meeting transcript (.txt, .vtt, .srt); the server sends it to the AI
  11: provider configured by the company admin (Anthropic Claude, OpenAI, Kimi, or
  12: GLM, using the company's own API key) and returns a draft summary and
  13: suggested action items that the member reviews before anything is saved.
  14: This delivers roadmap requirements MEET-01..MEET-07 plus Markdown export.
  15: MEET-08 (assignment notifications and due-date reminders) moves to Phase 7.
  16: 
  17: ## 2. Problem statement
  18: 
  19: Meetings happen in Virtual Office spaces, but nothing they decide is kept in
  20: the product: there is no way to write notes, track who owes what, or find a
  21: past decision. The database already has `meeting_notes` and
  22: `meeting_note_action_items` tables and an unused repository, but users have no
  23: write path and no UI. The dashboard shows an "AI Meeting Notes" card that leads
  24: nowhere. Teams lose decisions and follow-ups, and the product cannot show its
  25: AI meeting-notes promise to investors.
  26: 
  27: ## 3. Goals and success metrics
  28: 
  29: ### Goals
  30: 
  31: - G1: Members can use meeting notes in real daily work, at production quality.
  32: - G2: The transcript-to-draft AI flow is polished enough to demonstrate to investors.
  33: - G3: Each company uses and pays for AI with its own provider key; the platform never holds subscription 
credentials.
  34: 
  35: ### Success metrics
  36: 
  37: - SM-1: A one-hour transcript (≤1 MB) produces a reviewable draft within 60 seconds with the configured real 
provider.
  38: - SM-2: Search returns the first page within 1 second with 1,000 notes in the company.
  39: - SM-3: Zero notes, versions, action items, transcripts, or AI settings of another company are ever visible or 
modifiable.
  40: 
  41: ## 4. Users and stakeholders
  42: 
  43: ### Primary users
  44: 
  45: - Company member: creates notes, edits notes they authored or participate in, completes action items assigned to 
them, uploads transcripts, searches, exports.
  46: - Company admin (`role = admin`): everything a member can do, plus edits and deletes any note of the company and 
configures the company's AI provider, key, model, reasoning level, and daily limit.
  47: 
  48: ### Secondary users
  49: 
  50: - Participant: a member listed on a note; may edit it.
  51: - Assignee: a member assigned an action item; may mark it complete.
  52: 
  53: ### Stakeholders
  54: 
  55: - Product owner (Giuliano): runs acceptance against the online database; demonstrates the feature to investors.
  56: 
  57: ## 5. Scope
  58: 
  59: ### In scope
  60: 
  61: - Notes per space: create, view, edit, delete; rich-text content; participants.
  62: - Version history with view and restore; concurrent-edit conflict detection.
  63: - Action items with assignee, due date, and completion.
  64: - Transcript upload (.txt, .vtt, .srt), AI draft summary and suggested action items, review-before-save, 
reprocess.
  65: - Company AI settings (admin): provider among Anthropic, OpenAI, Kimi, GLM; API key; model and reasoning level; 
daily limit.
  66: - Full-text search with space and period filters.
  67: - Markdown (.md) export of a note.
  68: - "Notes" tab in the space detail and a "Meetings" page; the dashboard "AI Meeting Notes" card links to the 
Meetings page.
  69: - Notes survive deletion of their space.
  70: 
  71: ### Out of scope
  72: 
  73: - MEET-08 assignment notifications and due-date reminders (moved to Phase 7, Announcements, which builds the 
shared notification center and scheduler).
  74: - Sign-in with a ChatGPT/Codex or Claude subscription (Anthropic prohibits it for third-party apps; OpenAI's 
"Sign in with ChatGPT" is a partner-only preview). Future item, contingent on OpenAI granting access.
  75: - PDF export, note templates, calendar integration, sharing notes outside the company.
  76: - Automatic recording or transcription of in-app audio calls.
  77: - Real-time collaborative editing.
  78: - Background AI processing that survives leaving the page.
  79: - Semantic (embedding) search; searching inside transcript text.
  80: - Per-user model choice (admin-only setting).
  81: 
  82: ## 6. User journeys
  83: 
  84: - J1 Manual note: member opens a space's Notes tab → "New note" → enters title, date, participants, content → 
saves → note appears in the tab and on the Meetings page.
  85: - J2 Action items: an editor adds items with description, optional assignee and due date; the assignee or an 
editor marks an item complete.
  86: - J3 Revise: an editor edits and saves; a new version is recorded. If someone saved in between, the editor sees 
a conflict warning and the newer content before deciding.
  87: - J4 History: a viewer opens the version list (author, time), opens an old version; an editor restores it, 
creating a new version.
  88: - J5 AI from transcript: member chooses "New note from transcript" (or "Attach transcript" on an existing note) 
→ selects a file → sees the notice naming the AI provider → starts processing → reviews and edits the draft summary 
and each suggested action item (accept, edit, reassign, reject) → saves.
  89: - J6 Reprocess: an editor reprocesses the stored transcript, obtaining a new draft to review; the current 
summary changes only when the new draft is saved.
  90: - J7 Admin setup: admin opens company settings → AI → selects provider, enters API key, selects model and 
reasoning level, sets daily limit → saves; the key is then shown only masked.
  91: - J8 Search: member opens Meetings → types words, optionally filters by space and period → sees ranked results 
with matched excerpts → opens a note.
  92: - J9 Export: viewer clicks "Export Markdown", optionally ticks "Include transcript", and downloads a .md file.
  93: - J10 Delete: author or admin deletes a note after confirmation.
  94: 
  95: ## 7. Functional requirements
  96: 
  97: | ID | Requirement | Priority | Acceptance signal |
  98: |---|---|---|---|
  99: | FR-001 | A member creates a note in any space of their company with title (required, ≤200 chars), meeting date 
(required), participants (0–50 active company members chosen in a picker), and rich-text content (≤100,000 chars). | 
Must | AC-001, AC-002 |
 100: | FR-002 | The rich-text editor supports bold, italic, headings, bulleted and numbered lists, checklists, and 
links; stored and rendered content is sanitized so no script or unsafe URL runs. | Must | AC-003 |
 101: | FR-003 | The space detail has a "Notes" tab listing that space's notes, newest meeting date first. | Must | 
AC-001 |
 102: | FR-004 | A "Meetings" page lists all notes of the company with search and filters; the dashboard "AI Meeting 
Notes" card links to it. | Must | AC-014, AC-015 |
 103: | FR-005 | A note view shows title, date, space (or "Space removed"), participants, content, summary, action 
items, the stored transcript, and access to version history and export. | Must | AC-001, AC-019 |
 104: | FR-006 | The author, current participants, and admins can edit a note; others cannot. | Must | AC-004 |
 105: | FR-007 | Saving a note that changed since the editor loaded it is rejected with a conflict warning showing the 
newer version; nothing is overwritten silently. | Must | AC-005 |
 106: | FR-008 | Every successful save creates an immutable version of title, meeting date, participants, content, and 
summary, recording who saved and when. | Must | AC-006 |
 107: | FR-009 | Any viewer can list versions and open an old one; an editor can restore it, which creates a new 
version; action items are not changed by restore. | Must | AC-006 |
 108: | FR-010 | The author or an admin can delete a note after confirmation; deletion permanently removes the note, 
its versions, action items, and transcript. | Must | AC-007 |
 109: | FR-011 | Note editors create, edit, and delete action items (description required; one optional assignee who 
is an active company member; optional due date without time); editors and the assignee can toggle completion; at most 
100 items per note. | Must | AC-008, AC-009 |
 110: | FR-012 | A member uploads a .txt, .vtt, or .srt transcript of at most 1 MB, either to create a new note or to 
attach to an existing note they can edit; a note holds one transcript and a new upload replaces it. | Must | AC-010 |
 111: | FR-013 | Empty, oversized, wrong-extension, or unreadable (non-text) files are rejected before any AI call 
with a clear message; nothing is saved and nothing counts toward the daily limit. | Must | AC-011 |
 112: | FR-014 | .vtt and .srt cue numbers and timestamps are removed before processing; speaker names are kept. | 
Must | AC-012 |
 113: | FR-015 | Before processing, the user sees a notice naming the provider that will receive the transcript text. 
| Must | AC-010 |
 114: | FR-016 | The server sends the transcript to the company's configured provider and returns a draft containing a 
summary (key points, decisions, next steps) in the transcript's language and suggested action items with suggested 
assignees. | Must | AC-010 |
 115: | FR-017 | A suggested assignee is linked only when it matches exactly one active company member; otherwise the 
item is unassigned. | Must | AC-013 |
 116: | FR-018 | Nothing from the AI is stored until the user saves the reviewed draft; the user can edit the summary 
and accept, edit, reassign, or reject each suggested item. Saving stores the summary, accepted items, and transcript 
text and creates a version. | Must | AC-010 |
 117: | FR-019 | An editor can reprocess the stored transcript; the result is a new draft for review, and the current 
summary changes only on save. | Must | AC-010 |
 118: | FR-020 | If the provider fails, times out, refuses, or rejects the key, the note and transcript remain saved, 
the user sees an error with "Try again", and the attempt does not count toward the limit. | Must | AC-016 |
 119: | FR-021 | Leaving the page during processing discards the draft and the attempt does not count toward the 
limit. | Must | AC-016 |
 120: | FR-022 | An admin configures provider (Anthropic, OpenAI, Kimi, GLM), API key, model and reasoning level from 
a curated list per provider, and a daily limit (1–200, default 20). After saving, the key is shown only masked and is 
never returned to any browser. | Must | AC-017 |
 121: | FR-023 | Without a configured provider, manual notes work normally; AI controls are disabled with "An admin 
needs to configure AI"; admins also see a link to the settings. | Must | AC-018 |
 122: | FR-024 | The company's successful AI drafts are counted per day; when the limit is reached, processing is 
refused with a message stating the limit and when it resets. | Must | AC-016 |
 123: | FR-025 | Search matches words in title, content, summary, and action-item descriptions, ignoring accents and 
case, in Portuguese and English text, only within the caller's company; filters by space and meeting-date period; 
orders by relevance then meeting date; paginates; shows matched excerpts. | Must | AC-014 |
 124: | FR-026 | Export downloads a .md file with title, date, space, participants, content converted to Markdown, 
summary, and action items (assignee, due date, status); the transcript is included only when "Include transcript" is 
ticked. | Must | AC-019 |
 125: | FR-027 | Deleting a space keeps its notes; they show "Space removed" and remain on the Meetings page and in 
search. | Must | AC-020 |
 126: | FR-028 | A participant or assignee who left the company is shown as "Former member", keeps their place on the 
note, and loses all access. | Must | AC-021 |
 127: 
 128: ## 8. Business rules
 129: 
 130: | ID | Rule | Rationale |
 131: |---|---|---|
 132: | BR-001 | All members of a company can view all of its notes, versions, action items, and transcripts; nobody 
outside the company can. | Notes are company memory (r1 q4). |
 133: | BR-002 | Any member can create a note in any space of their company, including private spaces. | Notes can be 
written after the meeting (r2 q11). |
 134: | BR-003 | Edit rights: author, current participants, company admins. Delete rights: author, company admins. | 
r1 q5, r2 q11. |
 135: | BR-004 | Action items: note editors create/edit/delete; editors and the assignee toggle completion. | The 
assignee closes their own work (r2 q8). |
 136: | BR-005 | Participants and assignees must be active members of the note's company. | Prevents cross-company 
references. |
 137: | BR-006 | AI suggestions never become assignments or stored content without explicit user confirmation. | 
Avoids wrong attributions (r1 q12). |
 138: | BR-007 | Each save creates one immutable version; restore creates a new version; action items are 
current-state only. | r1 q8, r2 q10. |
 139: | BR-008 | The daily limit applies to every company, counts only successful drafts (including reprocessing), and 
is adjustable by admins from 1 to 200. | Cost and abuse protection (r1 q11, r2 q4). |
 140: | BR-009 | Only admins configure AI; the API key is write-only from the browser's perspective and is stored 
encrypted on the server. | r2 q1, q3. |
 141: | BR-010 | One transcript per note; a new upload replaces the stored text; the stored transcript is visible to 
every note viewer. | r1 q13, r2 q12. |
 142: | BR-011 | A save based on an outdated version is rejected. | No silent overwrite (r1 q18). |
 143: | BR-012 | AI access uses API keys only; no subscription credentials are requested, stored, or proxied. | 
Anthropic policy; OpenAI sign-in not available (r3). |
 144: 
 145: ## 9. Data and integrations
 146: 
 147: ### Data inputs
 148: 
 149: - Note fields, participants, rich-text content, action items, transcript files (.txt/.vtt/.srt, UTF-8 text, ≤1 
MB).
 150: - Admin AI settings: provider, API key, model, reasoning level, daily limit.
 151: 
 152: ### Data outputs
 153: 
 154: - Stored notes, versions, action items, transcripts, AI settings (key encrypted).
 155: - AI drafts (transient until saved).
 156: - Search results with excerpts; downloaded .md files.
 157: 
 158: ### Integrations
 159: 
 160: - Anthropic Messages API (official SDK), OpenAI API (official SDK), Kimi (Moonshot) and GLM (Zhipu/Z.ai) through 
their OpenAI-compatible endpoints. All calls are server-side from authenticated API routes.
 161: - Supabase Postgres (RLS, full-text search), Supabase Auth.
 162: - The existing `meeting_notes` and `meeting_note_action_items` tables and `IMeetingNoteRepository` are reused 
and extended (for example, `summary` becomes optional for manual notes, and `room_id` no longer cascades on space 
deletion).
 163: 
 164: ## 10. Constraints and assumptions
 165: 
 166: ### Constraints
 167: 
 168: - Next.js App Router, TypeScript strict, Supabase Postgres/Auth/RLS, TanStack Query, shadcn/ui, following 
AGENTS.md (server client in routes, `auth.getUser()`, `users.id` vs `users.supabase_uid`, roles `admin|member`).
 169: - API keys never reach client code, logs, error messages, or reports; transcript text is not logged.
 170: - AI calls only from the server; the browser never calls a provider.
 171: - Size limits: title ≤200 chars; content ≤100,000 chars; ≤50 participants; ≤100 action items per note; 
transcript ≤1 MB.
 172: - UI in the app's current language (English); AI summary in the transcript's language.
 173: - Migrations are written and applied locally during the phase; they are applied to the online database 
`vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` only at phase end with the owner's explicit authorization; rollout order is migrations then 
app, rollback is app first.
 174: - Only integration and E2E tests (no unit tests); one test process at a time; one final Chrome-only E2E run.
 175: 
 176: ### Assumptions
 177: 
 178: - The owner creates an OpenAI platform API key (billed separately from ChatGPT Plus/Pro) for the final test 
against the real provider (confirmed r3).
 179: - Automated tests use a local fake AI server compatible with the provider APIs (confirmed r2 q17).
 180: 
 181: ## 11. Edge cases and failure modes
 182: 
 183: | Case | Expected behavior |
 184: |---|---|
 185: | Two editors save the same note concurrently | The later save is rejected with a conflict warning showing the 
newer version (FR-007). |
 186: | Empty / >1 MB / wrong extension / binary file | Rejected before AI, clear message, nothing saved, not counted 
(FR-013). |
 187: | .vtt/.srt with cue numbers and timestamps | Stripped; speaker names kept (FR-014). |
 188: | Provider error, timeout, refusal, invalid key | Note and transcript stay saved; error + "Try again"; not 
counted (FR-020). |
 189: | User leaves the page while processing | Draft discarded; not counted (FR-021). |
 190: | AI suggests a name matching no member or several members | Item left unassigned for the user to choose 
(FR-017). |
 191: | Daily limit reached | Processing refused with limit and reset time (FR-024). |
 192: | No AI configured | Manual notes work; AI disabled with message; admin sees settings link (FR-023). |
 193: | Space deleted | Notes kept, labelled "Space removed" (FR-027). |
 194: | Participant/assignee leaves the company | Shown as "Former member"; no access (FR-028). |
 195: | Assignee is not a note editor | Can toggle completion of their item only (BR-004). |
 196: | Restore of an old version | New version created; action items unchanged (FR-009). |
 197: | Transcript contains instructions aimed at the AI | Treated as data; output limited to the draft fields; 
suggested assignees resolved only against the company member list. |
 198: | Pasted content with scripts or `javascript:` links | Removed by sanitization (FR-002). |
 199: | Member of company A requests a note, version, item, search, or setting of company B | Not found / denied 
(BR-001, SM-3). |
 200: | Non-admin calls the AI settings route | Denied (BR-009). |
